 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T05:15+08:00",
 "answer": "Singapore is experiencing significant haze due to regional forest fires, with the air quality in the **Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy** range. \n\nAccording to the latest updates from Singapore's National Environment Agency (NEA) (early morning on October 17, SGT / late evening October 16, UTC):\n\n**Early Morning Snapshot (as of 2:00 AM SGT, Oct 17):**\n* **24-hour PSI (Pollutant Standards Index):** **148 – 203** (Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy)\n* **1-hour PM2.5 Concentration:** **73 – 116 µg/m³** (Band 2 / Elevated)\n\n**Recent Regional Breakdown (from the 7:00 PM SGT reading on Oct 16):**\nTo give you an idea of how the haze is distributed across the island, the regional readings were:\n* **Central:** 24-hr PSI **225** *(Very Unhealthy)* | 1-hr PM2.5: **175 µg/m³** *(High)*\n* **West:** 24-hr PSI **202** *(Very Unhealthy)* | 1-hr PM2.5: **128 µg/m³** *(Elevated)*\n* **East:** 24-hr PSI **201** *(Very Unhealthy)* | 1-hr PM2.5: **132 µg/m³** *(Elevated)*\n* **South:** 24-hr PSI **183** *(Unhealthy)* | 1-hr PM2.5: **165 µg/m³** *(High)*\n* **North:** 24-hr PSI **162** *(Unhealthy)* | 1-hr PM2.5: **94 µg/m³** *(Elevated)*\n\n**Health Advisory:**\nBecause the readings are in the Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy bands, the NEA advises healthy individuals to reduce or avoid prolonged and strenuous outdoor physical exertion. Vulnerable groups—including the elderly, pregnant women, children, and those with chronic heart or lung conditions—should avoid all outdoor activity. \n\n*(Note: For immediate outdoor plans like going for a walk or jog, Singapore's NEA recommends checking the live **1-hour PM2.5** readings on the official [NEA website](https://www.nea.gov.sg) or the myENV app, as the 24-hour PSI reflects a longer-term average.)*",
